 "text": "Before costs, pooled: exit 12:00 +0.111, exit 15:00 +0.145, exit 18:00 +0.146, exit 21:00 (rule) +0.155, day from 17:00 New York +0.102; C1 takes about +0.016R a trade at 21:00. Windows with net R ≤ 0: day from 17:00 New York, strict open 2016 → (−1.021R). At 2x cost pooled: exit 12:00 +0.078, exit 15:00 +0.113, exit 18:00 +0.115, exit 21:00 (rule) +0.123, day from 17:00 New York +0.069. No alternative exit nets more than the rule's own 21:00 (+0.139R a trade): on gold the later the exit the more the trade keeps (USD/JPY peaked at 15:00 in usdjpy-vb-exit); the earlier exits keep most of it in both windows, so the result does not hang on the exact exit hour. The 17:00 New York day is the weak setting: positive in both windows but only +0.038R (month t +1.48) in 2016 →, worst drawdown −52.3R, so where the day is cut matters more than when it ends (18:00 and the New York day had been seen at $0.60 in gold-vol-breakout-k, 12:00 and 15:00 had not). Early fills (before 06:00 UTC): exit 12:00 +0.172R (n 756, t +4.01, holds), exit 15:00 +0.318R (n 756, t +5.43, holds), exit 18:00 +0.336R (n 756, t +5.20, holds), exit 21:00 +0.365R (n 756, t +5.34, holds); the late fills at 21:00 +0.082R (n 2957). The early split at 21:00 had been seen (holdout-vb-early, vb-early-exec), so none of these is new evidence. The 17:00 New York day's strict open keeps 1342 trades (+0.116R), almost none after 2015, when the feed's first candle of that day starts exactly 60 minutes after the anchor (the reason the inclusive reading is the main one). The early-fill combinations hold at 4 of 4 exits; early minus late is +0.124R (Welch t +2.62), +0.248R (Welch t +3.85), +0.261R (Welch t +3.70), +0.283R (Welch t +3.81) at 12:00 / 15:00 / 18:00 / 21:00. The early split was seen before on these years, so this is a hypothesis for the forward records (vb-early-forward), not a rule. Cautions: today's relative spread is charged on every past year (in 2007 a retail gold spread was surely wider), the 